sources:
    {"organization":"IAEA","title":"Radioisotopes in Medicine","url":"https://www.iaea.org/topics/radioisotopes"}

definition:
    An unstable isotope — a radioactive nuclide — that sheds excess energy by decaying until it reaches stability. Some are primordial leftovers from before Earth formed (uranium-238), some are continuously created by cosmic rays (carbon-14), and hundreds are manufactured in reactors and cyclotrons. Medicine leans on them heavily: technetium-99m lights up ~30 million diagnostic scans a year, iodine-131 treats thyroid disorders, cobalt-60 sterilizes equipment.
